WEBVTT

00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:03.136
Last video ended with this flat density, from

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:03.839
one to five.

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:05.356
We'll find its height soon.

00:00:06.385 --> 00:00:08.713
First, here's where flat densities come from.

00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:11.479
A bus comes every ten minutes, and you

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.823
turn up at a random moment, so every

00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:16.227
point in the ten-minute gap is equally likely.

00:00:16.911 --> 00:00:17.659
How long will you wait?

00:00:18.672 --> 00:00:21.297
Let's simulate two hundred arrivals, and sort their

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:22.861
waits into one-minute classes.

00:00:23.564 --> 00:00:25.002
The bars come out roughly level.

00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:27.569
Any wait from zero to ten minutes can

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.893
happen, and no stretch of waiting times is

00:00:29.954 --> 00:00:31.917
more likely than another of the same width.

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:34.067
So what does the density look like?

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.576
And how tall is it?

00:00:36.377 --> 00:00:38.892
Equally likely has a precise meaning here.

00:00:39.557 --> 00:00:41.524
Take any two intervals of the same width,

00:00:41.886 --> 00:00:43.934
say from one to three minutes and from

00:00:43.955 --> 00:00:44.496
six to eight.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.018
They must carry the same probability.

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:50.211
Slide a window of width two anywhere within

00:00:50.292 --> 00:00:53.182
zero to ten, and its probability can't change.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.060
On a density, probability is area.

00:00:56.927 --> 00:01:00.063
So equal widths with equal areas means equal

00:01:00.144 --> 00:01:00.465
heights.

00:01:01.148 --> 00:01:03.549
The density has to be flat: a constant

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.670
height c, from zero to ten, and zero

00:01:06.831 --> 00:01:09.732
outside, because a wait below zero or above

00:01:09.773 --> 00:01:10.713
ten can't happen.

00:01:11.569 --> 00:01:12.817
Now, how tall?

00:01:13.662 --> 00:01:16.556
The total area under any density is one.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.344
Here the area is a rectangle, ten wide

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:23.609
and c tall, so ten times c equals

00:01:23.831 --> 00:01:25.753
one, and c is one tenth.

00:01:26.695 --> 00:01:28.962
That height means a tenth of the probability

00:01:29.104 --> 00:01:29.665
per minute:

00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:32.354
each minute of the interval holds a tenth

00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:32.956
of the total.

00:01:33.941 --> 00:01:36.134
The same works on any interval from a

00:01:36.275 --> 00:01:36.637
to b.

00:01:37.281 --> 00:01:39.966
The rectangle is b minus a wide, so

00:01:40.048 --> 00:01:42.392
its height must be one over b minus

00:01:42.473 --> 00:01:42.512
a.

00:01:43.376 --> 00:01:44.411
We didn't choose that height.

00:01:44.736 --> 00:01:46.604
Total probability one forced it.

00:01:47.336 --> 00:01:49.328
So what's the chance you wait less than

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:49.872
four minutes?

00:01:50.497 --> 00:01:52.311
That's the area from zero to four.

00:01:53.078 --> 00:01:55.750
It's four wide and one tenth tall, which

00:01:55.812 --> 00:01:56.675
is zero point four.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.847
And between three and seven minutes?

00:01:59.832 --> 00:02:02.640
Also four wide, so also zero point four.

00:02:03.764 --> 00:02:06.410
For a uniform model, a probability is just

00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:08.114
the fraction of the interval that's covered.

00:02:08.857 --> 00:02:11.185
Where the interval sits doesn't matter, only its

00:02:11.247 --> 00:02:11.467
width.

00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.844
It's the same idea on any interval.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.741
If X is uniform from two to ten,

00:02:17.165 --> 00:02:18.250
the whole width is eight.

00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:20.503
So the chance X is between four and

00:02:20.544 --> 00:02:21.770
seven is three eighths.

00:02:22.435 --> 00:02:23.240
Here's a surprise.

00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:26.594
The narrower the interval, the taller the density

00:02:26.635 --> 00:02:27.056
has to be.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.769
What if X is uniform on a short

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.658
interval, from zero to a half?

00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:34.479
The rectangle is only a half wide.

00:02:34.984 --> 00:02:37.382
So to have area one, its height must

00:02:37.463 --> 00:02:37.926
be two.

00:02:39.012 --> 00:02:40.357
A density of two is fine.

00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:41.772
It isn't a probability.

00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:43.884
Probability is area,

00:02:44.406 --> 00:02:46.492
and the area is still exactly one.

00:02:47.236 --> 00:02:49.751
What about the cumulative distribution function?

00:02:50.396 --> 00:02:51.139
Back at the bus stop,

00:02:51.663 --> 00:02:53.412
F of t is the area to the

00:02:53.453 --> 00:02:53.814
left of t.

00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:57.067
As t moves right, the area grows by

00:02:57.088 --> 00:02:59.455
the same amount every minute, because the height

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:00.258
never changes.

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:03.767
So F climbs in a straight line, from

00:03:03.848 --> 00:03:05.912
zero at the start to one at the

00:03:05.993 --> 00:03:06.132
end.

00:03:06.935 --> 00:03:08.978
On any interval from a to b, F

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:11.383
of x is x minus a, over b

00:03:11.404 --> 00:03:11.964
minus a.

00:03:12.547 --> 00:03:14.938
Before a it stays at zero, and after

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:16.124
b it stays at one.

00:03:16.891 --> 00:03:18.268
You can check this with an integral too.

00:03:19.152 --> 00:03:20.876
F of x is the integral from a

00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:23.523
to x of one over b minus a,

00:03:23.845 --> 00:03:26.130
which gives the same x minus a, over

00:03:26.191 --> 00:03:26.972
b minus a.

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:29.747
Now back to the flat density from one

00:03:29.808 --> 00:03:30.289
to five.

00:03:30.971 --> 00:03:32.916
Say it's the time a train waits at

00:03:32.936 --> 00:03:35.883
a platform, uniform between one and five minutes.

00:03:36.624 --> 00:03:39.354
The interval is four wide, so the density

00:03:39.474 --> 00:03:41.943
is one quarter from one to five, and

00:03:42.024 --> 00:03:42.867
zero elsewhere.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:43.869
Then F

00:03:44.371 --> 00:03:47.018
of t is the integral of a quarter

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:49.585
from one up to t, which is t

00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:50.828
minus one, over four.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.354
The chance the train waits more than three

00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:55.639
and a half minutes is one minus F

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:56.602
of three and a half.

00:03:57.264 --> 00:03:59.428
That's one minus two point five over four,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.954
which is zero point three seven five.

00:04:02.776 --> 00:04:04.198
And the chance it waits between two and

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:07.264
four minutes is three quarters minus one quarter,

00:04:07.285 --> 00:04:08.046
a half.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.794
That's two of the four minutes, so a

00:04:10.835 --> 00:04:11.557
half makes sense.

00:04:12.671 --> 00:04:13.684
This should feel familiar.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:16.733
A fair die spreads a total probability of

00:04:17.015 --> 00:04:18.978
one equally over six values,

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.963
so each gets one sixth.

00:04:21.664 --> 00:04:24.626
A continuous uniform spreads it evenly over an

00:04:24.747 --> 00:04:25.047
interval,

00:04:25.588 --> 00:04:27.529
so its height is one over the width.

00:04:28.392 --> 00:04:29.915
And because the density is flat,

00:04:30.498 --> 00:04:32.543
no value is higher than any other.

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:35.864
So a uniform distribution has no single mode.

00:04:36.557 --> 00:04:37.911
When is this a good model?

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:40.468
When nothing makes one part of the interval

00:04:40.509 --> 00:04:41.595
more likely than another.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.025
Turning up at a random moment for a

00:04:44.086 --> 00:04:46.776
bus that comes exactly every ten minutes fits.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.640
The rounding error, when you round a length

00:04:49.741 --> 00:04:51.881
to the nearest centimetre, often fits too,

00:04:52.623 --> 00:04:54.522
anywhere from minus a half to a half,

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:57.084
if the lengths aren't bunched near whole numbers.

00:04:58.049 --> 00:04:59.602
But a reaction time doesn't.

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:02.072
Those times bunched up in the middle, as

00:05:02.113 --> 00:05:02.874
the histogram showed.

00:05:03.738 --> 00:05:05.804
A flat model would give the extreme times

00:05:05.946 --> 00:05:07.109
far too much probability.

00:05:08.073 --> 00:05:10.180
And a bus wait doesn't fit either if

00:05:10.221 --> 00:05:12.648
you've checked the timetable, because then you'd arrive

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:13.632
just before the bus.

00:05:14.262 --> 00:05:15.231
Here's a way to remember it.

00:05:15.873 --> 00:05:16.174
Flat,

00:05:16.755 --> 00:05:17.897
so one over the width.

00:05:18.659 --> 00:05:20.847
And the probability is the width you want,

00:05:21.489 --> 00:05:22.373
inside the interval,

00:05:22.915 --> 00:05:23.979
over the whole width.

00:05:26.685 --> 00:05:27.134
Your turn.

00:05:27.723 --> 00:05:30.153
Write down the density, and find this probability.

00:05:30.675 --> 00:05:31.459
Pause here to answer.

00:05:42.101 --> 00:05:45.187
The interval is five wide, so f of

00:05:45.427 --> 00:05:48.213
x is one fifth between three and eight,

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:49.635
and zero elsewhere.

00:05:50.437 --> 00:05:52.722
From four to six point five is two

00:05:52.762 --> 00:05:55.708
point five wide, so the probability is two

00:05:55.769 --> 00:05:58.114
point five over five, which is a half.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.074
A height of one eighth is wrong.

00:06:01.478 --> 00:06:03.371
The width is eight minus three,

00:06:03.895 --> 00:06:04.237
not eight.

00:06:05.925 --> 00:06:06.313
Last one.

00:06:06.943 --> 00:06:07.668
Find k,

00:06:08.171 --> 00:06:09.459
then find the probability.

00:06:10.286 --> 00:06:11.277
Pause here to answer.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:23.788
More than six minutes is the part from

00:06:23.888 --> 00:06:24.792
six up to k.

00:06:25.314 --> 00:06:28.309
So k minus six, over k, is zero

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.113
point two five.

00:06:29.656 --> 00:06:31.638
Then k minus six is a quarter of

00:06:31.698 --> 00:06:34.460
k, so three quarters of k is six,

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:35.601
and k is eight.

00:06:36.382 --> 00:06:38.638
So P of X less than three is

00:06:38.718 --> 00:06:39.242
three eighths.

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:42.033
Six over k equals zero point two five

00:06:42.154 --> 00:06:42.695
is wrong.

00:06:43.077 --> 00:06:45.027
That's the chance of less than six minutes,

00:06:45.431 --> 00:06:46.978
and it gives k equals twenty-four.

00:06:48.721 --> 00:06:51.025
So a uniform density is flat,

00:06:51.626 --> 00:06:53.870
its height is forced to be one over

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:54.271
the width,

00:06:54.892 --> 00:06:57.437
and its probabilities are fractions of the interval.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.529
Next, a new question.

00:07:00.192 --> 00:07:02.278
Think of the train's flat density as a

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:03.703
shape resting on a beam.

00:07:04.307 --> 00:07:05.201
Where would it balance?

00:07:06.008 --> 00:07:08.113
And for a density that isn't flat, how

00:07:08.134 --> 00:07:09.858
do we find the balance point, and the

00:07:09.899 --> 00:07:10.219
spread?

00:07:12.761 --> 00:07:13.625
Here are the key notes.

00:07:14.027 --> 00:07:15.575
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:15.616 --> 00:07:16.078
to copy them.
